  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nTonight...Early morning showers have persisted well into the  \nafternoon here, limiting the atmosphere from recovering as much. \nThat said, the combination of some clearing out west and \nincreasing moisture has allowed a pool of greater instability to\nform across western and north-central MN. As the front moves \nsoutheast this evening, we expect isolated to scattered storms \nto develop along it, most likely over central MN. Sufficient \nwind shear (~30 to 40 kts) and moisture (PWATS ~1.2 to 1.4\") \nwill support the development and organization of at least a few \nstorms. It is uncertain how many storms will become strong \nenough to reach severe limits given the relatively weaker mid \nlevel lapse rates (around 6 to 6.5 C/km). The SPC has \nmaintained a slight risk of severe weather across a strip of MN \nand northwest WI, with a focus on large hail. It is important to\nnote that not everyone within the risk area will see storms, in\nfact most may not see any rain, but that is the best \nenvironment for any storms that do develop to become more \norganized. The latest timing favors a later arrival for most \nlocations, with the initial storms developing around 5-7pm near\nAlexandria and Brainerd, then moving southeast towards the Twin\nCities metro and western WI around 9pm-midnight. Storms \nwest/south of I-94 are more uncertain for storm development, \nespecially across Minnesota.\n\nThis Weekend and Early Next Week...The forecast becomes \"boring\" \nagain heading into the weekend as drier air moves in and \nnorthwest flow continues aloft. Temperatures remain very \nseasonable, and cloud cover should be pretty limited to your \ntypical diurnal cumulus clouds each day. \n\nThings shake up a little bit as we head into next week and\nheights start to rise aloft. Surface winds shift out of the \nsouth and warmer temperatures and greater moisture gradually \nreturns. Long range guidance is showing broad agreement of rain \nchances at some point Monday-Wednesday, likely in the form of \nanother frontal passage. That's a ways out, so we'll focus on \nthe nice weather this weekend instead!",
